  "generalizedRange": "Middle and lower Yangtze River, China",
  "hook": "The Yangtze already lost its dolphin. Its porpoise is smiling back.",
  "story": [
    {
      "id": "porpoise-smile",
      "text": "The Yangtze finless porpoise has no fin on its back and no beak on its face — just a rounded grey head and a mouth that curves up into a permanent smile.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "wdc-species-guide",
        "riverdolphins-species-page"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-name",
      "text": "Locals call it the smiling river dolphin.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "riverdolphins-species-page"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-freshwater",
      "text": "It is the world's only freshwater porpoise, navigating the murk of the Yangtze by ultrasonic pulses.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "riverdolphins-species-page"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-baiji",
      "text": "It once shared the river with the baiji dolphin. The baiji was last seen in 2002, and declared functionally extinct within the decade.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "riverdolphins-atrisk-pdf"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-decline",
      "text": "The porpoise nearly followed: from about 2,700 in the early 1990s to barely a thousand.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "cas-porpoise-brink"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-pressures",
      "text": "The river itself was the problem — gillnets, ship noise drowning its sonar, sand mining, dams cutting the lakes away.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "wwf-2022-census-news",
        "cas-porpoise-brink"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-ban",
      "text": "Then China acted at river scale: a ten-year ban on all fishing across the Yangtze system, begun in 2021.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "riverdolphins-atrisk-pdf"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-rebound",
      "text": "The 2022 census found 1,249 — the first increase since records began.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "wwf-2022-census-news"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-reserves",
      "text": "In quiet oxbow reserves like Tian'ezhou, more than 150 porpoises now live and breed — roughly ten calves a year.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "cas-porpoise-brink"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "porpoise-witness",
      "text": "To witness the smiling porpoise is to ask that a river 400 million people depend on stay alive enough for it.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "wwf-2022-census-news"
      ]
    }
  ],
